      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by understanding the given condition and then use it to determine the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6.\n\nGiven that when \\( n \\) is divided by 6, the remainder is 1, we can express this as:\n\\[ n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6} \\]\nThis means that \\( n \\) can be written in the form:\n\\[ n = 6k + 1 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNext, we need to find the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6. First, we substitute the expression for \\( n \\) into \\( n + 2010 \\):\n\\[ n + 2010 = (6k + 1) + 2010 = 6k + 2011 \\]\n\nNow, we need to determine the remainder when \\( 6k + 2011 \\) is divided by 6. Notice that \\( 6k \\) is a multiple of 6, so it is already divisible by 6. Therefore, we only need to consider the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6. We do this by performing the division:\n\\[ 2011 \\div 6 = 335 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\nThis means:\n\\[ 2011 = 6 \\cdot 335 + 1 \\]\nSo, the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6 is 1. Therefore:\n\\[ 6k + 2011 \\equiv 1 + 0 \\pmod{6} \\]\nwhich simplifies to:\n\\[ 6k + 2011 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\nThus, the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]",
