      "text": "Let's start by analyzing the given information. We know that when \\( n \\) is divided by 6, the remainder is 1. This can be expressed mathematically as:\n\\[\nn \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n\\]\nThis means that \\( n \\) can be written in the form:\n\\[\nn = 6k + 1 \\quad \\text{for some integer } k\n\\]\n\nWe need to determine the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6. First, let's express \\( n + 2010 \\) in terms of \\( n \\):\n\\[\nn + 2010 = (6k + 1) + 2010 = 6k + 2011\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the remainder when \\( 6k + 2011 \\) is divided by 6. Notice that \\( 6k \\) is a multiple of 6, so it leaves a remainder of 0 when divided by 6. Therefore, we only need to consider the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6.\n\nTo find the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6, we perform the division:\n\\[\n2011 \\div 6 = 335 \\text{ remainder } 1\n\\]\nThis can be verified by calculating:\n\\[\n2011 = 6 \\times 335 + 1\n\\]\nThus, the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6 is 1. Since \\( 6k \\) leaves a remainder of 0, the remainder when \\( 6k + 2011 \\) is divided by 6 is the same as the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6, which is 1.\n\nTherefore, the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
